WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.519
Film Courage: You wrote a blog post (I think)
a few years back maybe that mentioned Tim

00:00:03.519 --> 00:00:07.660
Burton’s 1994 biopic on Ed Wood…excellent
film by the way.

00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:09.690
Scott Kirkpatrick: One of my favorites.

00:00:09.690 --> 00:00:15.150
Film Courage: Yeah…would you say this film
is really important because it shows still

00:00:15.150 --> 00:00:18.180
current examples of how films get made today.

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:20.620
Any thoughts…[films getting made] on a lower
level?

00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:27.550
Scott Kirkpatrick: You know, in truth those
budgets back then were massive today, like

00:00:27.550 --> 00:00:34.710
the kind of films that were getting made or
the Roger Corman-era of making films.

00:00:34.710 --> 00:00:43.200
One of the opening scenes of that movie (ED
WOOD), The Christine Jorgensen Story (that’s

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:50.190
what is was), it was basically an individual
back in the 50’s and some schlock producer

00:00:50.190 --> 00:00:52.950
was making a film about it.

00:00:52.950 --> 00:00:59.710
So Ed Wood sees the opportunity, walked into
the door, set up a meeting with Mr. Weiss

00:00:59.710 --> 00:01:03.699
and pitched himself as the guy who had to
make the movie.

00:01:03.699 --> 00:01:07.799
And because he was so enthusiastic because
in truth he was a human being but was very

00:01:07.799 --> 00:01:12.060
secretive about certain aspects of his life
and he just understood the compelling elements

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:15.319
of the story that had to be created, blah,
blah, blah, blah.

00:01:15.319 --> 00:01:19.299
And then, the producer’s response was more
or less “I don’t care.

00:01:19.299 --> 00:01:20.299
I need a producer.

00:01:20.299 --> 00:01:23.490
I need a guy who can make a film in three
days and make me a profit.

00:01:23.490 --> 00:01:24.819
That’s all I care about.”

00:01:24.819 --> 00:01:31.159
And the reason was, he’d already pre-sold
theaters and all of these states and needed

00:01:31.159 --> 00:01:33.609
the movie to cater to that.

00:01:33.609 --> 00:01:38.929
He needed a movie that was so many reels long
(using old film terminology), that was a certain

00:01:38.929 --> 00:01:42.479
budget level, that could be shot in a certain
amount of time and didn’t care about the

00:01:42.479 --> 00:01:43.479
quality.

00:01:43.479 --> 00:01:45.959
He just needed the product to fill-in the
gaps of where it was pre-sold.

00:01:45.959 --> 00:01:47.630
It still happens today.

00:01:47.630 --> 00:01:48.869
It’s no different.

00:01:48.869 --> 00:01:54.200
It’s the same concept in the indie space
today of the movies that get kind of pre-sold,

00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:58.919
pre-created, pre-developed and then they need
writers, producers, very creative-minded people

00:01:58.919 --> 00:02:02.889
to put that that together in a short period
of time and make it happen.

00:02:02.889 --> 00:02:07.369
That’s the real job of a creator is creating,
making things happen.

00:02:07.369 --> 00:02:14.260
Getting from Point A to Point B quickly and
effectively, that’s why at the studio level,

00:02:14.260 --> 00:02:20.180
major talents get tons of money and they receive
ridiculous sums of money for screenplays at

00:02:20.180 --> 00:02:25.000
the studio level because frankly it takes
a lot of work and it’s a very, very rare

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:28.319
talent to be able to produce on that level.

00:02:28.319 --> 00:02:31.090
So that’s kind of why it works that way.

00:02:31.090 --> 00:02:34.610
But if you haven’t seen it…for anyone
in the audience who hasn’t seen it [ED WOOD]

00:02:34.610 --> 00:02:38.730
it’s a great movie, very fun and in the
whole process throughout you actually learn

00:02:38.730 --> 00:02:42.200
a lot of great lessons about the filmmaking
process.

00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:47.819
And even though it’s from the 90’s and
talking about an era in the 50’s, the principles

00:02:47.819 --> 00:02:49.200
are exactly the same.

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:52.489
It still works the same way and always will
(I think).

00:02:52.489 --> 00:02:55.049
Film Courage: What about the Bela Lugosi character?

00:02:55.049 --> 00:02:57.110
Scott Kirkpatrick: Same concept.

00:02:57.110 --> 00:03:02.209
It’s an aging star, who frankly, was kind
of washed up at the time.

00:03:02.209 --> 00:03:07.879
But it’s the same idea of…I think even
in the opening scene that I’m talking about

00:03:07.879 --> 00:03:13.219
that was one of the things that he later went
back into the office to secure the job because

00:03:13.219 --> 00:03:18.500
he’d written a script in two days and he
had Bela Lugosi who was a star.

00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:23.410
So he basically said “If you need a schmuck
who can get you a movie in three days with

00:03:23.410 --> 00:03:25.040
a star.

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:26.040
Make me anything…”

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:29.890
I forget how he described it…”Anything
with a with a star, no matter if it’s good!”

00:03:29.890 --> 00:03:31.200
That’s how he described it.

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:36.200
And that’s basically Bela Lugosi was the
star who was at least a decent enough name

00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:43.620
that people recognized that elevated the product
just enough so it became a Bela Lugosi movie

00:03:43.620 --> 00:03:45.579
that was being distributed at that point.

00:03:45.579 --> 00:03:49.790
Film Courage: And last but not least, his
efforts to secure financing.

00:03:49.790 --> 00:03:54.299
So meeting the right person and having to
go to certain lengths to secure financing?

00:03:54.299 --> 00:03:59.510
Scott Kirkpatrick: So I think there’s a
massive misconception.

00:03:59.510 --> 00:04:06.959
When it comes to financing (as I kind of said)
movies are incredibly expensive to produce

00:04:06.959 --> 00:04:12.779
and people on the writing end, the directing
and producing end who want to get their work

00:04:12.779 --> 00:04:19.470
made seem to be very, very good at writing
and keeping a budget in mind and they’re

00:04:19.470 --> 00:04:23.750
really good at creating budgets and all the
great software that exists now but getting

00:04:23.750 --> 00:04:28.570
to that point of how is it going to be funded,
that’s sort of the complicated thing.

00:04:28.570 --> 00:04:33.340
In ED WOOD and I think the hopeful thing that
a lot of younger filmmakers hope is that they

00:04:33.340 --> 00:04:39.090
can go to a rich uncle or dentist and all
of this other stuff and just pool money together.

00:04:39.090 --> 00:04:45.580
Wherever money comes from and you can still
get movies off of the ground using that approach,

00:04:45.580 --> 00:04:52.200
at a higher level, if you’re creating content
that’s going to work for platforms out there,

00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:56.950
there are a lot of entities that will actually
invest the money and get that, so it’s kind

00:04:56.950 --> 00:05:03.300
of…I don’t know…I feel like I’m getting
a little lost in it…it’s not as cut and

00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:04.300
dry.

00:05:04.300 --> 00:05:10.440
It’s always a mystery as to where and how
funding comes together at the indie level

00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:13.099
because there is no one way to do it.

00:05:13.099 --> 00:05:19.090
But the truth is, if you are creating content
that actually has placement, that actually

00:05:19.090 --> 00:05:25.250
already has a spot to fill, it actually already
has room on a shelf to be placed, there are

00:05:25.250 --> 00:05:29.509
a lot of entities who are willing to invest
the money into it.

00:05:29.509 --> 00:05:34.440
I think the real answer is if you are going
out there to produce something that you want

00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:38.160
to produce because you’re passionate about
it and you’ve not done any real market research

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:42.000
or talked to companies about what the market
wants, you’re going to have a really tough

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:43.000
time getting financing.

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:47.710
If you take a step back you focus on what
the market needs, what it’s asking for,

00:05:47.710 --> 00:05:52.699
what companies are searching for and then
you cater to those needs, financing actually

00:05:52.699 --> 00:05:57.539
gets a lot easier because all of the sudden
there’s a lot of companies with deep pockets

00:05:57.539 --> 00:05:58.990
and they are looking to invest the money.

00:05:58.990 --> 00:06:02.780
They are just looking to invest it with stuff
that is going to help their goals.

